---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Community & Volunteer Leadership" current="/community-leadership/" description="Community and volunteer leadership experience from Richard J. Allen, including hospital chaplaincy, volunteer operations and communications, emergency-preparedness coordination, mentoring, technology adoption, and disaster-response service.">
<section class="community-hero"><div class="container community-hero-grid">
  <div>
    <div class="eyebrow light">Community & Volunteer Leadership</div>
    <h1>Service reinforces the same habits that make operations work.</h1>
    <p>Volunteer experience has strengthened listening, organization, communication, mentoring, confidentiality, continuity planning, and practical follow-through—without turning private service into public storytelling.</p>
    <div class="actions"><a class="button button-light" href="#service-in-practice">Explore service experience</a><a class="community-hero-link" href="/operations-leadership/">See professional leadership →</a></div>
  </div>
  <div class="service-bridge" aria-label="Volunteer habits that transfer to professional leadership">
    <div class="bridge-title">SERVICE → OPERATING HABITS</div>
    <div class="bridge-grid">
      <div class="bridge-node teal"><span>LISTEN</span><strong>Understand</strong><small>coaching · customers · difficult moments</small></div>
      <div class="bridge-node blue"><span>ORGANIZE</span><strong>Structure</strong><small>records · logistics · reporting · follow-through</small></div>
      <div class="bridge-node gold"><span>SUPPORT</span><strong>Stabilize</strong><small>service · preparedness · continuity</small></div>
      <div class="bridge-node violet"><span>COMMUNICATE</span><strong>Clarify</strong><small>training · coordination · next steps</small></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<section class="community-proof-band"><div class="container community-proof-grid" aria-label="Selected volunteer leadership proof">
  <div><strong>2012–2022</strong><span>documented volunteer operations & communications examples</span></div>
  <div><strong>2023–24</strong><span>regular Salem Health / Salem Hospital volunteer chaplain service</span></div>
  <div><strong>2012 → 2023</strong><span>multi-year emergency-preparedness and continuity evidence</span></div>
  <div><strong>3</strong><span>sanitized work-sample reconstructions from surviving records</span></div>
</div></section>

<section class="section" id="service-in-practice"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-heading-row"><div><div class="eyebrow">Service in practice</div><h2>Different settings, consistent leadership habits.</h2></div><p class="section-note">These experiences complement paid management work; they do not replace it.</p></div>
  <div class="grid two value-grid community-card-grid">
    <article class="card accent-teal">
      <div class="card-icon">CARE</div>
      <h3>Volunteer Chaplain · Salem Health / Salem Hospital</h3>
      <p><strong>July 2023–July 2024</strong></p>
      <p>Provided regular volunteer support in a hospital environment, strengthening skills in careful listening, discretion, confidentiality, compassionate communication, and appropriate coordination with staff.</p>
      <p><strong>Transferable strengths:</strong> emotional intelligence, calm communication, confidentiality, service, and supporting people through difficult moments.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="card accent-blue">
      <div class="card-icon">ADMIN</div>
      <h3>Volunteer Operations & Communications</h3>
      <p><strong>Long-running community administration · documented examples 2012–2022</strong></p>
      <p>Handled records and recurring reports, calendar and event coordination, audit and administrative follow-through, annual planning and material-order coordination, leadership communications, training/support for other volunteers, service-project logistics, recurring bulletins, shared-facility scheduling, website access and permissions, program-enrollment communications, and community event support. Also refined and handed off an annual cross-group reporting/history process after learning how to gather information more efficiently across multiple teams.</p>
      <p><strong>Transferable strengths:</strong> operations support, records/reporting, scheduling, recurring communications, training, stakeholder coordination, knowledge transfer, service-project logistics, basic website administration, and follow-through.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="card accent-gold">
      <div class="card-icon">MENTOR</div>
      <h3>Youth & Community Service</h3>
      <p><strong>Ongoing volunteer involvement</strong></p>
      <p>Participated in mentoring and community-service activities that involved planning, encouragement, teamwork, and helping others take greater ownership.</p>
      <p><strong>Transferable strengths:</strong> coaching, mentoring, planning, trust-building, and developing confidence in others.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="card accent-violet">
      <div class="card-icon">SERVE</div>
      <h3>Disaster-Response Service</h3>
      <p><strong>Florida · circa 2020</strong></p>
      <p>Volunteered in hurricane-cleanup work as part of a community recovery effort, contributing practical labor in a demanding environment.</p>
      <p><strong>Transferable strengths:</strong> teamwork, adaptability, service under difficult conditions, and willingness to help where needed.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</div></section>

<section class="section leadership-examples"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-heading-row"><div><div class="eyebrow">Selected leadership examples</div><h2>Service that required real coordination.</h2></div><p class="section-note">Specific examples are included only where the underlying record supports the work.</p></div>
  <div class="grid two value-grid">
    <article class="card accent-teal">
      <div class="card-icon">READY</div>
      <h3>Emergency Preparedness & Communication Continuity</h3>
      <p><strong>Documented examples · 2012, 2018 & 2023</strong></p>
      <p>Preparedness work progressed from proposing a structured way to capture emergency-relevant skills alongside community contact records, to initiating large-format area-map support for preparedness planning, to coordinating a 48-hour communication-readiness exercise with centralized reporting and follow-through.</p>
      <p><strong>Transferable strengths:</strong> continuity planning, information organization, distributed communication, preparedness support, reporting, and follow-through.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="card accent-blue">
      <div class="card-icon">ADOPT</div>
      <h3>Community Technology Adoption</h3>
      <p><strong>2025</strong></p>
      <p>Planned and coordinated a community website-adoption effort, added upcoming event information to the site, worked with other facilitators, and organized a support approach in which younger members could help people less comfortable with the technology.</p>
      <p><strong>Transferable strengths:</strong> change adoption, stakeholder coordination, training planning, hands-on implementation, and user support.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
  <div class="callout" style="margin-top:1.5rem"><strong>Additional hands-on service:</strong> organized volunteer move-assistance projects and supported public-facing charitable donation events, helping visitors with questions and the donation experience.</div>
</div></section>

<section class="section toolkit-section"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-heading-row"><div><div class="eyebrow">Evidence, not just claims</div><h2>A sanitized look at the operating system behind the service.</h2></div><p class="section-note">These are privacy-safe reconstructions from contemporaneous working records—not screenshots of private files. Names, contact details, household information, and unnecessary religious content are intentionally omitted.</p></div>

  <div class="toolkit-grid">
    <article class="toolkit-card">
      <div class="artifact-top"><span class="artifact-year">2018</span><span>Administrative playbook</span></div>
      <div class="artifact-paper" aria-label="Sanitized reconstruction of an administrative playbook">
        <div class="artifact-line"><b>MEETINGS</b><span>Record decisions + assignments</span></div>
        <div class="artifact-line"><b>FOLLOW-UP</b><span>Send action owners clear next steps</span></div>
        <div class="artifact-line"><b>REPORTING</b><span>Compile recurring quarterly reports</span></div>
        <div class="artifact-line"><b>TRAINING</b><span>Onboard and coach new volunteers</span></div>
        <div class="artifact-line"><b>COVERAGE</b><span>Build backup when an owner is unavailable</span></div>
      </div>
      <h3>Role clarity + follow-through</h3>
      <p>Working documentation captured recurring responsibilities, reporting expectations, training, deadlines, delegation, and continuity when someone could not complete an assignment.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="toolkit-card">
      <div class="artifact-top"><span class="artifact-year">2017</span><span>Recurring planning cadence</span></div>
      <div class="artifact-paper cadence-preview" aria-label="Sanitized reconstruction of a recurring planning cadence">
        <div class="cadence-row"><span>NOW</span><strong>Plan current month</strong></div>
        <div class="cadence-arrow">↓</div>
        <div class="cadence-row"><span>NEXT</span><strong>Plan one month ahead</strong></div>
        <div class="cadence-arrow">↓</div>
        <div class="cadence-row"><span>YEAR</span><strong>Maintain an annual sequence</strong></div>
        <div class="cadence-note">Adjust the sequence as monthly needs change.</div>
      </div>
      <h3>From ad hoc to repeatable</h3>
      <p>A planning document converted recurring program work into a visible cadence: current-month decisions, next-month preparation, fixed recurring elements, and an annual sequence that could be adjusted over time.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="toolkit-card">
      <div class="artifact-top"><span class="artifact-year">2021</span><span>Recurring communications</span></div>
      <div class="artifact-paper bulletin-preview" aria-label="Sanitized reconstruction of a recurring community bulletin">
        <div class="bulletin-title">WEEKLY COMMUNITY BULLETIN</div>
        <div class="bulletin-rule"></div>
        <div class="bulletin-block"><strong>PROGRAM</strong><span>Current-week information</span></div>
        <div class="bulletin-block"><strong>EVENTS</strong><span>Upcoming dates and changes</span></div>
        <div class="bulletin-block"><strong>SCHEDULES</strong><span>Recurring service information</span></div>
        <div class="bulletin-deadline">Submission deadline: Thursday</div>
      </div>
      <h3>Structured communication rhythm</h3>
      <p>Recurring bulletins combined current program information, event notices, schedules, and a clear submission deadline—evidence of routine information intake, editing, and distribution rather than one-off messaging.</p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <div class="callout toolkit-callout"><strong>Why these examples matter:</strong> they show the same operating habits visible in my professional work—turn ambiguity into structure, make ownership clear, build repeatable rhythms, communicate changes, and keep work moving when people or circumstances change.</div>
</div></section>

<section class="section tight"><div class="container">
  <div class="privacy-banner"><strong>Privacy by design</strong><span>The professional value is the pattern—listening, organizing, mentoring, communicating, protecting trust, and helping groups move through real-world needs.</span><small>Private pastoral details, names of people served, youth identities, household information, and unnecessary religious specifics are intentionally omitted.</small></div>
</div></section>

<style>
  .community-hero{padding:4.7rem 0 4rem;color:#fff;background:radial-gradient(circle at 82% 18%,rgba(183,121,31,.18),transparent 24%),linear-gradient(128deg,#102f55 0%,#15486b 50%,#0f6769 100%)}.community-hero-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1.05fr) minmax(390px,.95fr);gap:3rem;align-items:center}.community-hero h1{font-size:clamp(2.8rem,6vw,5rem);line-height:1;letter-spacing:-.05em;margin:.7rem 0 1.2rem}.community-hero p{font-size:1.12rem;color:#e5eef4;max-width:700px}.community-hero-link{color:#fff;font-weight:800;text-decoration:none;align-self:center}.service-bridge{padding:1.2rem;border-radius:24px;background:rgba(255,255,255,.07);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.18);box-shadow:0 24px 55px rgba(0,0,0,.16)}.bridge-title{font-size:.7rem;font-weight:900;letter-spacing:.15em;color:#ccece7;margin:.15rem 0 .9rem}.bridge-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:.7rem}.bridge-node{--node:#0f766e;min-height:135px;padding:1rem;border-radius:16px;background:rgba(255,255,255,.08);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.13);display:flex;flex-direction:column;justify-content:center}.bridge-node span{font-size:.66rem;font-weight:900;letter-spacing:.11em;color:#d5e7ef}.bridge-node strong{font-size:1.25rem;margin:.3rem 0;color:#fff}.bridge-node small{color:#d5e2ea;line-height:1.35}.community-proof-band{background:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)}.community-proof-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr)}.community-proof-grid>div{padding:1.25rem;border-right:1px solid var(--border)}.community-proof-grid>div:last-child{border-right:0}.community-proof-grid strong{display:block;font-size:1.55rem;line-height:1;color:var(--teal)}.community-proof-grid span{display:block;margin-top:.4rem;color:var(--muted);font-size:.84rem;line-height:1.35}.leadership-examples{background:linear-gradient(180deg,#f7faf9,#fff)}.community-card-grid .card{transition:transform .18s ease,box-shadow .18s ease}.community-card-grid .card:hover{transform:translateY(-2px);box-shadow:0 14px 32px rgba(23,32,42,.07)}
  .toolkit-section{background:linear-gradient(180deg,#f5f8fc 0%,#f7f8fa 100%);border-top:1px solid var(--border);border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)}
  .toolkit-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr));gap:1rem}.toolkit-card{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:18px;padding:1rem;box-shadow:0 12px 28px rgba(23,32,42,.06)}.toolkit-card h3{margin:1.1rem 0 .45rem}.toolkit-card p{color:var(--muted);margin:0}.artifact-top{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:.7rem;margin-bottom:.75rem;color:var(--muted);font-size:.84rem;font-weight:750}.artifact-year{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;padding:.2rem .55rem;border-radius:999px;background:var(--blue-soft);color:var(--accent);font-weight:850}.artifact-paper{min-height:275px;border:1px solid #d7dee7;border-radius:12px;padding:1rem;background:linear-gradient(180deg,#fff 0%,#fbfcfd 100%);box-shadow:0 8px 20px rgba(23,32,42,.08);position:relative;overflow:hidden}.artifact-paper::after{content:"SANITIZED RECONSTRUCTION";position:absolute;right:-42px;bottom:22px;transform:rotate(-35deg);font-size:.62rem;letter-spacing:.12em;font-weight:850;color:rgba(21,62,117,.14)}.artifact-line{display:grid;grid-template-columns:82px 1fr;gap:.7rem;padding:.67rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid #edf1f4;position:relative;z-index:1}.artifact-line:last-child{border-bottom:0}.artifact-line b{font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.06em;color:var(--accent)}.artifact-line span{font-size:.86rem;color:#455463;line-height:1.35}.cadence-preview{display:flex;flex-direction:column;justify-content:center;gap:.2rem}.cadence-row{display:grid;grid-template-columns:54px 1fr;align-items:center;gap:.65rem;padding:.68rem;border:1px solid #e5eaf0;border-radius:10px;background:#fff;position:relative;z-index:1}.cadence-row span{font-size:.68rem;font-weight:850;letter-spacing:.08em;color:var(--teal)}.cadence-row strong{font-size:.92rem}.cadence-arrow{text-align:center;color:#94a3b8;font-weight:900;line-height:1;position:relative;z-index:1}.cadence-note{font-size:.76rem;color:var(--muted);text-align:center;margin-top:.35rem;position:relative;z-index:1}.bulletin-preview{padding:1.15rem}.bulletin-title{font-size:.78rem;font-weight:900;letter-spacing:.08em;color:var(--accent);position:relative;z-index:1}.bulletin-rule{height:3px;background:linear-gradient(90deg,var(--accent),var(--teal));margin:.55rem 0 1rem;border-radius:999px;position:relative;z-index:1}.bulletin-block{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:.7rem;padding:.62rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid #edf1f4;position:relative;z-index:1}.bulletin-block strong{font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.06em;color:var(--gold)}.bulletin-block span{font-size:.83rem;color:#455463;text-align:right}.bulletin-deadline{margin-top:1rem;padding:.55rem .65rem;border-radius:8px;background:var(--gold-soft);color:#75500f;font-size:.78rem;font-weight:800;position:relative;z-index:1}.toolkit-callout{margin-top:1.4rem}.privacy-banner{display:grid;gap:.3rem;padding:1.2rem 1.35rem;border-radius:18px;background:var(--teal-soft);border-left:5px solid var(--teal)}.privacy-banner span{color:#344854}.privacy-banner small{color:var(--muted)}
  @media(max-width:950px){.community-hero-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}.service-bridge{max-width:700px}.community-proof-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr}.toolkit-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}.artifact-paper{min-height:auto}.toolkit-card{padding:1.15rem}}
  @media(max-width:600px){.community-hero{padding:3.4rem 0}.bridge-grid,.community-proof-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}.community-proof-grid>div{border-right:0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)}.community-proof-grid>div:last-child{border-bottom:0}.artifact-line{grid-template-columns:72px 1fr}.bulletin-block{display:grid}.bulletin-block span{text-align:left}}
  @media(prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){.community-card-grid .card{transition:none}.community-card-grid .card:hover{transform:none}}
</style>
</BaseLayout>
